      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s oldest continuously operating hotel, the Avery Hotel Restaurant & Saloon, opened in 1858 and still serves travelers today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avery%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before settlers, Northern Sierra Miwok and Washoe peoples used the oak woodlands near the Stanislaus River for acorn gathering and seasonal camps.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/avery_california",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After a 1946 fire destroyed most buildings, the community rebuilt around the historic hotel, turning it into a logging supply hub during the postwar boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/avery_california",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Avery sits on State Route 4, a winding road that climbs the Sierra Nevada foothills, offering panoramic views of oak and pine forests.",
